%abc-2.1
% Battle Hymn of the Republic -- pinned reference transcription (not an arrangement)
%
% SOURCE EDITION
%   "Battle Hymn of the Republic. Adapted to the favorite Melody of 'Glory, Hallelujah,'
%   written by Mrs. Dr. S. G. Howe, for the Atlantic Monthly."
%   Boston: Oliver Ditson & Co., 277 Washington St., 1862. Plate number 21454.
%   "Entered according to act of Congress AD 1862 by O. Ditson & Co. in the Clerks Office
%   of the Dist. Court of Mass." Deposit note on the title page: "Deposited April 9th 1862".
%   No composer or arranger is named anywhere in the edition.
%   Library of Congress, Music Division, M1640 .B; https://www.loc.gov/item/2023782802/
%   Scans (LoC storage JP2), sha256:
%     001.jp2 title page  8417617dcb72c4fa2b8973058a7dd11826d31c4b3f161d4186b394b97a12441d
%     002.jp2 p. 3        e1e046c7d4418cb1551cb98ffba02679cf4ff260264acd4b99de0b0bc941145c
%     003.jp2 p. 4        7c13c1d18e352686e19403a9a7a74ddce752dbfbe882c1342d4da013676d2162
%     004.jp2 p. 5        0a803fc07998d4cdaec5c877e90a157cd25f7740dae8ca8b1bd4492308010b99
%     https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/public/music/muscivilwar-200000858/00N.jp2
%
% WHAT IS TRANSCRIBED
%   X:1 = pp. 3-4: piano introduction (bars 1-3), verse 1 (bars 4-12), chorus (bars 13-20).
%   X:2 = p. 5: the music printed for verses 2-5 (9 bars), which ends "Chorus." (back to p. 4).
%   Every printed note of the vocal staves and both piano staves is here; nothing is added.
%   Voices:
%     MEL  the melody: bars 1-3 = the piano RH (the introduction has no voice part);
%          from bar 4 = the vocal line (soprano in the chorus).
%     RH   piano right hand exactly as printed (it duplicates MEL in bars 1-3).
%     LH   piano left hand exactly as printed; its lowest note is the bass line.
%     CH   the chord of each beat, as spacer rests carrying a chord symbol. Derived by the
%          transcriber from melody+RH+LH; chord symbols are not printed in the edition.
%          "F7" beats: the A is in the melody (A4), the accompaniment has F-C-Eb.
%          "Bb/F" in bar 3 beat 3: only F2 (LH) and Bb4 (melody) sound; read as the
%          cadential 6/4 that bars 11, 19 and p5-8 print in full (F3-Bb3-D4 over F2).
%          "N.C." = beats where only the repeated F4 drum figure sounds.
%     ALT  chorus alto (second treble staff of p. 4); rests elsewhere.
%     BAS  chorus bass voice (bass-clef vocal staff of p. 4); rests elsewhere.
%
% CONVENTIONS
%   L:1/16, so every length is an integer number of sixteenths: 1 = sixteenth,
%   2 = eighth, 3 = dotted eighth, 4 = quarter, 6 = dotted quarter, 8 = half, 16 = bar.
%   M:C is printed (common time); every bar of every voice sums to exactly 16 (checked).
%   K:Bb applies to every voice; "=B" is the one printed accidental (B natural, bar 2).
%   ABC octaves: C = C4 (middle C), c = C5, C, = C3, C,, = C2, B,,, = Bb1.
%   Ties: bar 11 and p5-8 print an arc over two C5s under one syllable ("truth"/"day");
%   it is read as a tie (c4-c3). Slurs and staccato dots are copied where printed.
%   Bar 16: one arc spans the first two notes of all three voices under "lu-"; it is a slur
%   in the soprano (F4-D4) and joins repeated pitches in alto (D4 D4) and bass (Bb2 Bb2),
%   copied as a slur everywhere -- a singer holds those repeated notes as one.
%   No tempo word and no dynamics are printed.
%
% SOURCE LOCATION OF EACH BAR: see the comment on every bar line below
%   (page, scan file, system, position in the system).
%
% THIS EDITION'S ACCOMPANIMENT BEFORE 1862
%   The piano introduction (bars 1-3) and the verse accompaniment pattern (bars 4-5 checked)
%   are already printed in Ditson's "The popular refrain of Glory, Hallelujah as sung by the
%   Federal Volunteers" (Boston: Oliver Ditson & Co., 1861; LoC item 2023783074; title page
%   note "Deposited July 24th 1861"), also anonymous; the 1861 print adds "f" in bar 1 and a
%   cautionary flat on the Bb4 of bar 3. Ditson reissued the 1862 plates about 1890 (LoC item
%   2023871344; page 3 system 1 is identical); its "[Steffe, Wm.]" is a pencil note on the title
%   page, not print.
%
% VARIANTS AMONG EARLY EDITIONS (transcribed here: Ditson 1862)
%   - 1859 American Sunday-School Union, The Prayer-Meeting Tune-Book, tune "Say, Brothers"
%     (Arr. by A. T.): 2 flats, cut time, no upbeat ("Say," on the downbeat), hymn rhythm;
%     "Glo-ry, glo-ry, hal-le-lu-jah" is a stanza sung to the same music, not a refrain.
%   - 1859 A. S. Jenks, Devotional Melodies, "Say, Brothers, will you Meet Us?"
%     (Arranged by E. P. Resteen): 2/4, "Not too fast", dotted figures.
%   - 1861 Ditson, Glory, Hallelujah: 2 flats, common time, same piano introduction as here;
%     words "John Brown's body" / "Ellsworth's body".
%   - 1861 Russell & Patee, John Brown's Song ("Music by Phillip Simonds"): C major, common time.
%   - 1862 Ditson (this file): 2 flats, common time. Upbeat: one eighth ("Mine") in verse 1,
%     two sixteenths ("I have") in the p.5 music for verses 2-5. Chorus "Glo-ry! Glo-ry":
%     F4 dotted quarter + Eb4 eighth, then dotted-eighth/sixteenth pairs (bar 13).
%     The p.5 music (X:2) differs from verse 1 (X:1 bars 4-12) in three places only:
%     p5-1, the upbeat bar (voice F1F1 for "I have"; piano four F3-Bb3-D4 chords over Bb2,
%     where bar 4 has Bb4 + three chords over Bb3-D4 + three Bb2); p5-3 (d3 c1 d3 c1,
%     where bar 6 has d3 d1 d3 c1); and p5-9, whose LH rests are printed z4 z8 (z4 z4 z4 in bar 12).

